{"id":3349,"date":"2021-07-28T17:24:06","date_gmt":"2021-07-29T00:24:06","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3349"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:49:16","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:49:16","slug":"functions-2-4","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/functions-2-4\/","title":{"rendered":"2.4: Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566d80\" name=\"6a22150566d80\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a function?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code in one named set. For a real-world example, when you\u2019re drinking a glass of water, the entire task might look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Find glass<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bring over to water source<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pour water into glass<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make sure water doesn\u2019t overflow!<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drink water<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">But instead of saying all of that, you could just say you\u2019re drinking water! Functions work the same way. Multiple lines of code can be included in one function that you name and set up to be used in your program.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566eb2\" name=\"6a22150566eb2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Defining a function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To define a function, or explain what is going to be in it, you will need the \u2018to do something\u2019 block in the pink functions menu. Drag that to your workspace and then name your function in the white box. This should be something memorable that describes your function well. Next, drag the blocks you need into the function.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3355&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it now! Make a function that has the name \u201cblink\u201d that has Zumi go forward and then blink.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3357&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3358&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566f08\" name=\"6a22150566f08\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Calling a function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you define a function, you should see a block for it in the functions menu. To use it, drag it into your workspace and drop it to wherever you would like to see it in your program.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3360&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566f64\" name=\"6a22150566f64\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Defining functions with parameters<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What if we want the function to do something different depending on the parameters we provide? Remember that you can change the number of seconds Zumi drives forward. You can also add parameters to your functions! In order to add parameters to your function in Blockly, click on the gear icon and attach an input block to the inputs block. The input can then be accessed from the variables tab.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3361&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3362&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Warning: <\/strong>If you plan on using your function later on, do not remove it from your workspace! Doing this will delete the entire function nd you will not be able to locate it in the functions menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566fb0\" name=\"6a22150566fb0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Zumi Rectangle<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Write a function that makes Zumi drive in a rectangle pattern using two parameters (one of the long sides and one for the short sides). Try using the function with different parameter values and see what kinds of rectangles you can come up with!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Once you&#8217;ve made your rectangle function, try comparing it to either of the solutions below! Remember, when you want to call a custom function make sure that you drag the function block onto your workspace after defining the function.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]The first solution makes Zumi drive in a rectangle shape using right turns:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3428&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;][vc_column_text]The second solution makes Zumi drive in a rectangle shape using left turns instead of right turns:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3429&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150566ff6\" name=\"6a22150566ff6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Review<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this lesson, you learned how to use the function blocks to define functions and call them in your program! The function block is helpful for when you have commonly-used code that repeats often, and can be used to make your code smaller and more efficient.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 What is a function? [vc_column_text]Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code in one named set. For a real-world&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/functions-2-4\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2897,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":2878,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2108,2131,2122,2126,2230],"class_list":["post-3349","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-senior","lesson-tag-zumi","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3349","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[